---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Reuse and citation — Gourmet Gastronomer"
  description="Original Gourmet Gastronomer prose is CC BY 4.0. How to attribute a page, and what this license does not cover."
  permalink="/reuse/"
  crumbs={[{ name: "Reuse and citation", url: "/reuse/" }]}
>
  <main class="home" id="main">
    <p class="eyebrow">Reuse</p>
    <h1>Reuse and citation</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Original encyclopedia prose is offered so it can be quoted, taught, and
      built on — with credit.
    </p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Original Gourmet Gastronomer writing, site copy, and original diagrams
        are licensed under
        <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International</a>
        (CC BY 4.0). You may copy, redistribute, and adapt that material,
        including commercially, if you give attribution.
      </p>
      <p>
        Third-party sources cited in articles remain under their own copyright.
        A quotation or a bibliographic line is not a license to reproduce the
        source.
      </p>
      <h2>How to attribute</h2>
      <p>
        Name the work, the canonical URL, the stable graph id (<code>gg:…</code>),
        and the page’s <code>updated</code> date when you are citing a specific
        version. Article pages include a Cite this page block with a recommended
        string plus Chicago and APA forms.
      </p>
      <p>
        Example: Gourmet Gastronomer. "Desired dough temperature."
        https://gourmetgastronomer.com/baking/desired-dough-temperature/
        gg:topic:desired-dough-temperature.
      </p>
      <h2>What this is not</h2>
      <p>
        Atlas pages are knowledge records, not a permission to republish a
        producer’s branding. Food-safety process times must still be checked
        against the authoritative tested process we cite — copying our summary
        does not replace that source.
      </p>
      <p>
        See also <a href="/editorial/">Editorial standards</a>,
        <a href="/method/">Method</a>, and
        <a href="/corrections/">Corrections</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
